.TH PERP 6 "9th April 1992".SH NAMEperp \- a multiscreen diamond collection game.SH SYNOPSIS.I perp[ level_num ].SH DESCRIPTIONThe idea of.I perpis to collect all the diamonds necessary to advance to the next level. Havingdone this you will be presented with a small end of level screen, and the newscreen..spDiamonds can be found either lying around, or manufactured from crushingthe lighter coloured boulders together. The darker @-filled bolders cannot becrushed into diamonds, only moved around..spTo move around use the h,j,k and l keys..brThere is a save and load feature which can be invoked using S and L keys. Thesave file is $HOME/cod.save. (This is one of the less buggy features of thegame - so please make good use of it).brTo quit and restart a level, press 'q'. To redraw the screen, press control L.To exit the game press control C (or interrupt)..SH OPTIONS.Iperp \fBlevel_num\fP.PPThe start level is level number 0. To start on another level simply state thenumber as the first (and only) argument to.I perp..SH FILESFiles are used for the sprites and the map data. These are by default.I LIBDIR/mapand.I LIBDIR/sprwhere LIBDIR is usually /usr/games/lib/perp..SH AUTHOR.I perpwas written by James 'Rince' Bonfield..SH BUGSThere are quite likely to be thousands of bugs. However, the author wishes tostress that the game is not likely to be updated and corrected any more thanit's current state. Feel free to fix things yourself :-).spThere are no checks on which levels exist. If you attempt to start on a nonexistant level then the game will simply go loopy. This will also happen uponfinishing the last level..spAlso, the level argument can be supplied as anegative number. This, also understandably causes a few worried looks from theprogram. If you try harder then it'll probably just have a nervouse breakdownand burst into tears with a segmentation fault..spVery little (in fact none) integrity checking is done on the map and spritedata files read in by the game.